<h2>The trap</h2>
<p>The appellant, a Junior Engineer and Inspector with the Delhi Vidyut Board at Nand Nagri, was alleged to have demanded Rs 10,000 from the complainant in connection with an electricity theft case concerning his father&rsquo;s premises. In a CBI trap laid on 19 December 2000, Rs 3,000 in phenolphthalein-treated notes changed hands — but the hand that took them belonged to one Ram Kumar, whose hand-wash and pocket-wash tested positive. The appellant had left the spot and was arrested three days later. At trial, Ram Kumar was discharged at the charge-framing stage; the appellant alone was convicted in 2007. His appeal waited nineteen years for hearing.</p>

<h2>Attribution, not possession</h2>
<p>The appeal&rsquo;s central argument wrote itself: no recovery from the accused, no phenolphthalein on his hands, the receiver discharged. The court&rsquo;s answer reframes the enquiry. Demand and acceptance are the essential ingredients, and mere recovery is insufficient — but the demander need not be the person from whose possession the money is recovered. The question is attribution: &ldquo;the physical recovery from Ram Kumar does not sever the connection between the demand made by the Appellant&rdquo;. The complainant and the shadow witness established that the appellant demanded the money and directed its handover to Ram Kumar; the receipt, recovery and chemical tests formed one continuous chain.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The statutory anchor:</strong> Explanation 2 to Section 7 reaches gratification obtained through another person. The court relied on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent exposition in <em>State by Lokayuktha Police v. K. Rangayya</em>, 2026 SCC OnLine SC 963, holding demands routed through intermediaries squarely within Section 7.</p>
</div>

<h2>The evidentiary framework</h2>
<p>The judgment works within the Constitution Bench&rsquo;s framework in <em>Neeraj Dutta v. State (NCT of Delhi)</em>, (2023) 4 SCC 731: demand must be proved, but it may be proved circumstantially, and &ldquo;acceptance of gratification may be established from the circumstances&rdquo;. The older line — <em>B. Jayaraj</em>, <em>T. Shankar Prasad</em>, <em>Sarwan Singh</em> — was applied for the propositions that recovery alone does not convict, and that credible testimony of demand plus a completed handover does. The acknowledged deficiencies in the audio recording (differing transcripts, an unsigned and unexamined transcriber, no specimen voice samples) were weighed and found non-fatal, because the conviction never rested on the tape.</p>

<h2>The defences that failed</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The discharge of Ram Kumar.</strong> A co-actor&rsquo;s discharge settles nothing about the principal&rsquo;s own demand. Liability under Section 7 attaches to the demander whoever holds the notes.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Clean hands, literally.</strong> No phenolphthalein on the appellant was consistent with the case against him — he directed the payment; he did not touch it.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sanction.</strong> Absent a demonstrated non-application of mind, a sanction order under Section 19 is not unsettled by argument alone.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Alibi.</strong> The defence witness placed the appellant with him only until about 4 p.m. — leaving the trap hours unaccounted for. An alibi must cover the time of the offence completely.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the case matters now</h2>
<p>Trap cases built in the era of the unamended PC Act continue to reach judgment decades later, and this decision illustrates how the modern demand-and-acceptance doctrine applies to them. For the defence, it marks the limits of the recovery-centric argument: once credible evidence of demand exists, distance from the physical notes is no sanctuary. For investigators, it validates the careful use of shadow witnesses and wash tests on every hand in the chain. And for public servants facing such allegations, it underlines that the serious work of a PC Act defence lies in confronting the evidence of demand — the element the prosecution must always prove, and the one on which these cases are truly won or lost.</p>
